## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # You Can Save Water By... – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Environment and Recycling</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> You Can Save Water By...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present findings from a group discussion. Interactive: Engage in problem-solving conversations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present findings from a group discussion on sustainable living, comparing past and current practices through examples of daily habits (e.g., "Before, people threw everything away, but now they sort items for recycling and composting.")
Can engage in problem-solving conversations about sustainability by asking and responding to questions (e.g., "How can we save water?" "We can turn off the tap.")
Can ask and answer questions about sustainable practices, showing recognition of key discussion points (e.g., "What happens if we don't recycle?" "If we don't recycle, there will be more waste, and the water, soil, and atmosphere will be more polluted.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activates prior knowledge and introduces the lesson's communicative context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of different water-saving actions (e.g., someone turning off the tap, collecting rainwater, using a bucket for washing a car) and water-wasting actions (e.g., leaving the tap running, long showers, watering plants with a hose). Students quickly identify and name the actions. Teacher asks: "What do you see?" "Is this good for the `environment`?" "Can you `save` water by doing this?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences comparing past and present water habits and giving advice.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "In the past, people often left the tap running when brushing teeth. Now, we `should` turn off the tap. We `should` `save` water `carefully`." (Focus on stress contrast: *left* vs. *should turn off*).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "Before, I `wasted` water when washing dishes. Now, I `protect` this `resource` and turn off the tap. If I `waste` water, there `will be` less for everyone." (Pronunciation focus on /t/ in 'wasted', /d/ in 'saved').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modal 'should' and the first conditional.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'You `should` `recycle` plastic bottles,' is that a suggestion or an obligation?" (Expected answer: A suggestion/advice.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What happens if we `waste` water?" (Expected answer: There `will be` less water; the `planet` `will` suffer.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Environment and Recycling". Students are exposed to target language in a meaningful context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated dialogue between two teenagers, Maria and Juan, discussing water conservation. The dialogue contrasts past family habits with current `environmental` practices, using the target vocabulary and grammar (past simple vs. present practices, first conditional, 'should').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students understand conversations about daily routines and advice related to `environmental` care.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Excerpt:</b>
                    <br><i>Maria:</i> "My grandmother told me that in the past, her family `often` `wasted` a lot of water. They didn't think about saving `energy` or `resources`."
                    <br><i>Juan:</i> "Really? That's different from now. Today, my family tries to `save` water. My dad says, 'If we `waste` water, there `will be` less for the `planet`.' We `should` `clean` the car with a bucket, not a hose."
                    <br><i>Maria:</i> "That's a good idea! My mom says we `should` `recycle` water from washing vegetables for our plants. It `protects` the `environment` and reduces `waste`."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue (teacher reads or plays audio) and answer gist questions orally.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is Maria's grandmother's family's past habit regarding water?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What does Juan's dad say about `wasting` water?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What advice does Maria's mom give about vegetable water?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking. Students practice using the target language with guidance to build confidence and correctness.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking about past vs. present practices, giving advice, expressing cause-effect. Uses grammar: Past simple vs. present practices, First conditional, Modal 'should'. Vocabulary: `recycle`, `waste`, `environment`, `pollution`, `energy`, `planet`, `resource`, `reduce`, `reuse`, `clean`, `save`, `protect`, `environmental`, `important`, `carefully`, `often`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> The teacher provides incomplete sentences on the board or screen, and students complete them orally, focusing on the correct verb tense and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In the past, people didn't `______` (recycle/protect) their `waste`." (Expected: `recycle`)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, we `should` `______` (save/waste) water `carefully`." (Expected: `save`)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If we `don't clean` the `environment`, there `______` (will be/is) more `pollution`." (Expected: `will be`)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You `______` (should/can) turn off the lights to `save` `energy`." (Expected: `should`)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice - Substitution Drill (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. Teacher gives a model sentence: "In the past, we `wasted` water. Now, we `save` it." Students substitute the bolded verbs and nouns with other vocabulary related to `environment` and `recycling` from a list (e.g., `polluted` the `planet`, `cleaned` the `resource`, `recycled` `waste`). Each pair creates 3-4 new sentences.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "Before, they `polluted` the `planet`. Today, they `protect` the `environment`."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "In the past, he `didn't recycle`. Now, he `sorts` `waste` for `energy`."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Pronunciation focus:* Teacher monitors and reinforces correct pronunciation of past tense "-ed" endings: /d/ in "saved," /ɪd/ in "wanted," and /t/ in "watched," "wasted," "protected," "polluted."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Advice Chain:</b> Students form pairs. One student makes a statement about a problem related to water `waste` or `pollution`. The other student gives advice using 'should' and/or a first conditional sentence. They then switch roles.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "People `often` leave the tap running when washing hands."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "They `should` turn off the tap. If they `save` water, the `planet` `will be` `cleaner`."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Sometimes, people throw old batteries `in` the regular `waste` bin."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "They `should` `recycle` them `carefully`. If they `don't recycle` batteries, they `will pollute` the `environment`."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario. Focus is on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion and Presentation - "Our Community's Water Action Plan"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Group Formation:</b> Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Discussion:</b> Each group discusses common ways people `waste` water in their homes or community (past and present). They collaboratively propose solutions to `save` water and `protect` the `environment`. They `should` use all target grammar structures (past simple vs. present, first conditional, 'should') and vocabulary (e.g., `resource`, `pollution`, `energy`, `clean`, `recycle`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Discussion Prompts:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What `did` people do `in the past` that `wasted` water?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are some current water-`waste` problems `in` our `environment`?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How `can we save` water?" (We `should`...)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What `will happen if we don't protect` this `important resource`?" (If we `don't save` water, there `will be` more `pollution`.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Plan Creation:</b> Groups create a simple "Water Action Plan" with 3-4 proposed solutions. They prepare to present their findings verbally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Presentation:</b> Each group presents their "Water Action Plan" to the class. Students take turns speaking, explaining past problems and present solutions, using contrasting phrases ("In the past...", "Today...", "Before...", "Now we..."). Other students listen and can ask questions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">*Sociolinguistic Competence:* Students collaborate in problem-solving group sessions to propose solutions to `environmental` issues, using English as the medium for discussion and presentation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check achievement of learning outcomes and provide feedback. Includes student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist (focus on speaking fluency, accuracy of target grammar and vocabulary, and collaboration).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their group's "Water Action Plan." The teacher observes each student's participation in the group discussion and their verbal presentation of findings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially of past tense verbs) and clear delivery.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (`recycle`, `waste`, `environment`, `save`, `protect`, etc.).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of `past simple vs. present practices`, `first conditional`, and `modal should`.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of ideas when presenting problems and solutions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active collaboration and engagement in group problem-solving.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral or written on a small slip of paper):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new way you learned to `save` water or `protect` the `environment` today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well `did` you use the phrases comparing 'past' and 'present'?" (e.g., "I used them well / sometimes / need more practice.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Allows for student and teacher reflection on the lesson, learning, and future adjustments.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (oral):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most `important` thing you discussed about `saving` water today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident are you now `in` giving advice about `environmental` issues `in` English?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this `Speaking` lesson? (e.g., student engagement in group discussions, use of target grammar/vocabulary). The group presentations were lively, and most students attempted to use the first conditional and 'should' for advice.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still struggle with the correct `-ed` pronunciation for past simple verbs; targeted drilling might be needed. More specific prompts for comparing past and present could reinforce the contrast. Ensure all students get speaking time.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: This speaking practice will directly prepare students for `Lesson 4: Writing Composition`, where they `will write` short paragraphs describing `past` and `present` water-saving habits and proposing solutions for a `cleaner` `planet`. The vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced today `will be` `important` for their written output.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>